  "hints": [
    "Which events start a session? The first of each user, and any event that comes a long time after the previous one. How can a row see the previous row?",
    "`LAG(at) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY at, id)`; mark a start as 1, anything else as 0.",
    "A running `SUM` of the marks, per user, gives the session number. Then `GROUP BY user_id, session`. Compare gaps in whole seconds (`strftime('%s', at)`)."
  ],
  "solution": "WITH marked AS (\n  SELECT user_id, id, at,\n         CASE\n           WHEN LAG(at) OVER w IS NULL THEN 1\n           WHEN CAST(strftime('%s', at) AS INTEGER) - CAST(strftime('%s', LAG(at) OVER w) AS INTEGER) >= 1800 THEN 1\n           ELSE 0\n         END AS starts\n  FROM events\n  WINDOW w AS (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY at, id)\n),\nnumbered AS (\n  SELECT user_id, at, SUM(starts) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY at, id) AS session\n  FROM marked\n)\nSELECT user_id, session, COUNT(*) AS events, MIN(at) AS started_at, MAX(at) AS ended_at\nFROM numbered\nGROUP BY user_id, session\nORDER BY user_id, session;\n",
  "explanation": "**The idea.** Sessionising is two window functions. First, mark every event that **starts** a session: the user's first event, or one that comes at least 1800 seconds after the previous one (`LAG(at)` gives the previous event's time, `NULL` for the first). Second, a **running sum** of those marks numbers the sessions: it stays the same inside a session and goes up by one at each start.\n\n**Why seconds.** `strftime('%s', ...)` turns a timestamp into whole seconds, so the gap comparison is exact. Subtracting `julianday` values (fractions of a day) and multiplying back to minutes can land at 29.999999 for a gap of exactly 30 and split sessions wrongly.\n\n**The pattern** (mark the starts, running-sum them, group by the sum) is the standard answer to \"group consecutive rows\": sessions, streaks of consecutive days, runs of the same status."
}
